.\" tvmail-backend(1) man page.
.TH TVMAIL\-BACKEND 1 "September 2026" "tvmail 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tvmail\-backend \- the part of tvmail that does the actual work
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tvmail\-backend
.I subcommand
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tvmail\-backend
is a small, standard\-library\-only Python program that performs every
mail\-format operation on behalf of
.BR tvmail (1):
listing mailboxes, decoding MIME, composing message skeletons, handing outgoing
mail to
.BR sendmail (8),
deleting, marking, saving drafts, and expanding
.I ~/.mailrc
aliases.
.PP
.BR tvmail (1)
runs it as a child process and reads its output.
It is documented \(em and packaged \(em separately because it is perfectly
usable on its own from a shell or a script; the Turbo Vision front end holds no
mail logic that this does not.
.PP
It runs in one of two
.BR modes .
On the host that owns the mail store it reads mbox files and hands outgoing
mail to
.BR sendmail (8)
(\fBlocal\fP mode).
On any other machine it is an IMAP/SMTP client of that host (\fBremote\fP
mode): the same subcommands, but backed by
.BR imaplib / smtplib .
See
.B MODES
below.
.PP
Each subcommand prints its result to standard output and exits
.B 0
on success,
.B 1
on a usage or not\-found error, or
.B 2
on a runtime failure.
.SH "MAILBOX ARGUMENT"
Wherever a subcommand takes a
.I MBOX
argument it accepts either a filesystem path or one of these shorthands:
.TP
.B spool
.I $MAIL
or
.IR /var/mail/$USER .
.TP
.B mbox
.IR ~/mbox .
.TP
.B drafts
.IR $folder/drafts
(default
.IR ~/Mail/drafts ),
where
.I $folder
is
.B "set folder"
from the mailrc files.
.TP
.B sent
.IR $folder/sent ,
or the mailrc
.B "set record"
value.
.B send
files a copy here.
.TP
.B spam
.IR $folder/Junk
(remote:
.BR Junk ).
Where
.B pull
files server\-tagged spam.
.TP
.B trash
.IR ~/.local/share/tvmail/trash.mbox .
.TP
.B dead
.I $DEAD
or
.IR ~/dead.letter ,
treated as a single\-message mailbox.
.PP
In
.B remote
mode the shorthands map to IMAP folders instead \(em
.BR spool " \(->" " INBOX" ,
.BR mbox " \(->" " Archive" ,
.BR trash " \(->" " Trash" ,
.BR drafts " \(->" " Drafts" ,
.BR sent " \(->" " Sent" ,
.BR spam " \(->" " Junk"
\(em overridable in
.IR tvmail.conf .
.B dead
is always the local file.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.BI "list " [MBOX]
One line per message:
.IR "index" ,
tab,
.I flags
.RB ( N
new or
.B .
seen), tab,
.IR date ,
tab,
.IR from ,
tab,
.IR subject .
A mailbox that does not exist yet lists as empty.
.TP
.BI "show " "IDX " [MBOX]
Decoded headers followed by the best available text of message
.IR IDX .
.TP
.BI "raw " "IDX " [MBOX]
The message exactly as stored.
.TP
.BI "parts " "IDX " [MBOX]
One line per MIME part:
.IR "part\-number" ,
.IR content\-type ,
.IR filename ,
.IR bytes .
.TP
.BI "save " "IDX PARTNO DEST " [MBOX]
Write the decoded payload of one MIME part to
.IR DEST .
.TP
.BI "delete " "IDX " "\fR[\fPIDX ...\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--mbox " "MBOX\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--trash " "DEST\fR]\fP"
Remove one or more messages.
With
.BR \-\-trash ,
each is appended to
.I DEST
first.
A
.I .bak
of the mailbox is kept.
.TP
.BI "purge " "\fR[\fPMBOX\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fPfilters\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--to-folder " "DEST" " | --expunge\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP-n\fR]\fP"
Move every message in
.I MBOX
that matches
.B all
of the given filters \(em substring (case\-insensitive) on
.BR \-\-from ", " \-\-subject ", " \-\-to ,
an
.B \-\-older\-than
age in whole days, and/or
.BR \-\-seen / \-\-unseen
\(em into
.I DEST
(a mailbox shorthand or path; default
.BR trash ).
.B \-\-expunge
deletes them instead.
At least one filter is required; with none it refuses to run.
.B \-n
(\c
.BR \-\-dry\-run )
lists what would move and changes nothing.
Handy from
.BR cron
on the master:
.RS
.EX
tvmail\-backend purge --from "Cron Daemon" --older-than 14
tvmail\-backend purge --subject "***SPAM***" --to-folder spam
.EE
.RE
.TP
.BI "mark " "IDX " "read|unread " [MBOX]
Set or clear the Status flag
.RB ( \(rs Seen
over IMAP).
.TP
.BI "compose\-template " "\fR[\fP--to " "A\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--subject " "S\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--in-reply-to " "IDX\fR]\fP " [MBOX]
Print a draft skeleton: a
.BR From: ,
.BR To: ,
.B Subject:
block, a blank line, and (for a reply) the quoted original.
.TP
.BI "send " "\fR[\fP--from " "A\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--to " "A ...\fR]\fP " "\fR[\fP--subject " "S\fR]\fP"
Read an RFC\ 822 message (or a bare body, with
.BR \-\-to )
on standard input, submit it (\c
.BR sendmail (8)
locally, SMTP in remote mode) and file a copy in
.BR sent .
.B alias
and
.B group
names in
.BR To: ,
.B Cc:
and
.B Bcc:
are expanded from the mailrc files before delivery.
A blank
.B "[folders] sent"
in
.I tvmail.conf
disables the copy.
.TP
.B save\-draft
Read an RFC\ 822 message on standard input and append it to the
.B drafts
mailbox, adding a
.B Date:
header if absent.
.TP
.B aliases
Print the address book:
.I name
tab
.IR "expanded, addresses" ,
one per line.
.TP
.B pull
In
.B local
mode: run
.B pop\-pull
(if on
.IR PATH ),
then sweep the inbox for spam.
In
.B remote
mode: just the spam sweep \(em the master does the fetching.
The sweep moves inbox messages whose
.B Subject:
contains the
.B "[spam] subject"
tag (default
.BR ***SPAM*** ,
what SpamAssassin prepends) into the
.B spam
folder.
.B "[folders] spam"
blank turns it off.
.TP
.B ping
Print
.BR pong .
A liveness check.
.TP
.B serve
Persistent mode.
Read one request (a normal argv) per line on standard input and write a framed
reply per request:
.RB ` "<status> <nbytes>" '
and a newline, then
.I nbytes
bytes.
The mbox / MIME machinery \(em and, in remote mode, the IMAP connection \(em
stay warm, so
.BR tvmail (1)
pays start\-up once instead of once per keystroke.
.B send ", " save\-draft ", " pull
and
.B serve
itself are refused inside the loop.
.SH MODES
The mode is chosen by
.I ~/.config/tvmail/tvmail.conf
(an INI file;
.B $TVMAIL_CONF
overrides the path):
.RS
.EX
[service]
mode   = auto | local | remote
master = host1 ; host2

[imap]   host= port=993 ssl=true verify=true cafile= user=
[smtp]   host= port=587 starttls=true auth=true from= user=
[folders] saved=Archive  trash=Trash  drafts=Drafts
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B mode
=
.B auto
(the default) resolves to
.B local
when this host's name is listed in
.BR master ,
or when there is no
.B [imap]
section; otherwise
.BR remote .
.B $TVMAIL_MODE
overrides it for one run.
.PP
Passwords are never kept in
.IR tvmail.conf .
They are read from
.I ~/.netrc
.RI ( "machine <host> login <user> password <pw>" ,
mode 0600) or from
.B $TVMAIL_IMAP_PASS
/
.BR $TVMAIL_SMTP_PASS .
With
.B "[smtp] auth = false"
(a relay that trusts the LAN) no password is needed;
.B "[smtp] from"
forces the sender identity so the master relays without rewriting.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tvmail/tvmail.conf
Selects local vs. remote and holds the IMAP/SMTP host settings.
See
.BR MODES .
A commented
.I tvmail.conf.example
ships with the source.
.TP
.I ~/.netrc
The IMAP/SMTP password, keyed by host.
Mode 0600.
.TP
.I /etc/mailrc ", " ~/.mailrc
Read for
.BR "set folder" ,
.BR "set DEAD" ,
and
.BR alias / group .
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/sendmail
The delivery agent used by
.B send
in local mode.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TVMAIL_CONF
Path to
.I tvmail.conf
(default
.IR ~/.config/tvmail/tvmail.conf ).
.TP
.B TVMAIL_MODE
Force
.B local
or
.B remote
for one run.
.TP
.B TVMAIL_IMAP_PASS ", " TVMAIL_SMTP_PASS
Passwords, in place of
.IR ~/.netrc .
.TP
.B MAIL ", " DEAD ", " MAILRC ", " SENDMAIL ", " XDG_DATA_HOME
Override the corresponding local\-mode defaults.
The spool falls back from
.I /var/mail
to
.IR /var/spool/mail ;
.B send
looks for
.BR sendmail (8)
then
.BR msmtp (1)
if
.B SENDMAIL
is unset.
